    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95481.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "I agree part. I feel like even before FTX, Democrats were more skeptical about crypto than Republicans or there were more skeptics in the Democratic Party than in the Republican Party. But the skepticism took on a new intensity after FTX. But again, the FTX effect could fade over time. Me, the main issue is not the reaction to, and I would argue that FTX strengthens the argument for a regulatory framework rather than maintaining a status quo that led to FTX in the first place. But the main stumbling block here are actually the regulators. I'm convinced we're not for Gary Gensler. We would have had a much poorer bipartisan market structure bill. We're not for the Federal Reserve. We would have a much broader bipartisan stable point bill. The regulators have been intent on sabotaging bipartisan cooperation around crypto, and it's been immensely frustrating to see firsthand.",
